<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lāčplēsis Day</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11 November</span> · <a href="/today-in-history/november-11">On this day: 11 November</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lāčplēsis Day is a Latvian day of remembrance held on 11 November for those who fell fighting for the country's independence. It commemorates the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pavel-bermondt-avalov">Pavel Bermondt-Avalov</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-russian-volunteer-army">West Russian Volunteer Army</a> at Riga in November 1919 and takes its name from Lāčplēsis, the bear-slaying hero of Andrejs Pumpurs's national epic. It is marked by candles placed along the walls of Riga Castle and at war memorials.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lacplesis-day.jpg" alt="Lāčplēsis Day" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Saeima · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saeimas_priek%C5%A1s%C4%93d%C4%93t%C4%81ja_kop%C4%81_ar_%C4%A3imeni_noliek_svec%C4%ABtes_11.novembra_krastmal%C4%81_(15581545787).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-council-of-latvia">People's Council of Latvia</a> proclaimed the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> in Riga on 18 November 1918, in a land overrun by the Imperial German Army. After the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, Allied forces ordered the German troops there to help the Latvian Army against the Bolshevik invasion of December 1918, but in April 1919 they turned on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a>. The Estonian Army, with the North Latvian Brigade, defeated them at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cesis">Battle of Cēsis</a> in June 1919. Former German and Russian imperial soldiers then invaded again in the autumn, and the outnumbered Latvian Army won on 11 November 1919.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At first the day honoured the 743 soldiers who fell in the Battle of Riga. The writer Kārlis Skalbe likened the army's fighting spirit to the spirit of Lāčplēsis, and the Constitutional Assembly created the Order of Lāčplēsis; on 11 November 1920 its chairman, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janis-cakste">Jānis Čakste</a>, presented the order to its first 288 knights in Esplanade Park in Riga. After the order ceased to be awarded on 1 November 1928, the day became a great army holiday of parades, feasts and concerts.</p><p>Under Soviet occupation from 1940 the celebration was strictly forbidden. It returned during the third awakening of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, and on 10 November 1989 the Supreme Soviet of the Latvian SSR made it a day of remembrance for the soldiers who died in the War of Independence. A proposal by Unity and the National Alliance in 2018 to make it a national holiday failed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Since 1988 people have placed candles by the wall of Riga Castle, and similar ceremonies take place in other towns, villages and military cemeteries. The day now honours all fallen Latvian freedom fighters, not only the knights of the order. Since 2007 many wear on the left chest a Latvian flag ribbon folded into a victory sign, introduced by the LNT television channel with the support of the Ministry of Defence, often throughout November.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lāčplēsis Day” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The defence of Riga against Bermondt-Avalov in October and November 1919, aided by Estonian armoured trains and Allied warships, is commemorated on <b>Lāčplēsis Day</b>, 11 November.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Republic of Latvia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
